INT. CAR FERRY - UPPER DECK DINING ROOM - NIGHT

A long cafeteria table bolted to the floor. Fluorescent lights. A view of black water. The ferry throbs beneath them.

Six members of the Abdullahi-Prewitt family sit with trays of congealed shepherd's pie. A funeral reception, after the funeral.

MATRIARCH ZAINAB ABDULLAHI-PREWITT, 70s, at the head. Her sons, CYRIL, 50s, and AUGUSTINE, 40s, glare across the table. Cyril's wife, PERPETUA, 50s, grips her napkin. Their daughter ROSAMUND, 20s, scrolls on her phone. And the youngest, THADDEUS, 16, keeps his eyes on the water.

ZAINAB
Eat. He would have wanted us to eat.

CYRIL
He wanted the boat.

AUGUSTINE
He wanted the boat sold.

CYRIL
He wanted it kept.

PERPETUA
Cyril, please.

AUGUSTINE
You never listened to him.

CYRIL
I listened. I just disagreed.

ROSAMUND
Can we not do this on a ferry?

THADDEUS
We're not leaving a ferry.

ZAINAB
Quiet, all of you.

They are not quiet.

AUGUSTINE
Dad's will says I sell it.

CYRIL
Dad's will says I decide.

PERPETUA
Both of you, stop.

AUGUSTINE
You always take his side.

PERPETUA
I take no side. I take the family's.

ROSAMUND
There is no family.

THADDEUS
I'm still here.

Silence. The ferry lurches over a wave. Trays slide. Zainab rises, steadies herself on the table.

ZAINAB
Your father's will is in my purse. He wrote it on a napkin.

CYRIL
A napkin?

ZAINAB
A napkin.

She opens her purse. Withdraws a folded, grease-spotted paper napkin. She sets it down. Flattens it. Everyone leans in.

ZAINAB (CONT'D)
"Give the boat to whoever sails it."

Silence.

ZAINAB (CONT'D)
That's all.

Thaddeus rises, slowly. He crosses to the window. He looks out into the dark at the glow of the wheelhouse.

THADDEUS
(quietly)
I know how.

The others look at him. For the first time all evening, nobody speaks.
